-- title:
--   Image of a Hopf kernel under a surjective bialgebra map
-- statement:
--   Let $k$ be a field, let $A$ be a commutative ring carrying a Hopf algebra structure over $k$ which is finite as a $k$-module and whose comultiplication is cocommutative, and let $B$, $C$, $D$ be commutative rings carrying bialgebra structures over $k$. Let $\pi : A \to B$, $\mu : A \to C$ and $\rho : B \to D$ be homomorphisms of $k$-bialgebras (maps that are simultaneously $k$-algebra and $k$-coalgebra homomorphisms), all three assumed surjective as functions, and suppose that the kernel of $\rho$, as an ideal of $B$, is the ideal generated by the image under $\pi$ of the kernel of $\mu$, i.e. $\ker \rho = \operatorname{Ideal.map} \pi (\ker \mu)$. For a bialgebra homomorphism the associated Hopf kernel [`HopfAlgebra.hopfKer`](def/HopfAlgebra_HopfKer.html#L19) is the subalgebra of coinvariants, the equalizer of $a \mapsto (\mathrm{id} \otimes \mu)(\Delta a)$ and $a \mapsto a \otimes 1$ inside $A \otimes_k C$, and similarly for $\rho$ inside $B \otimes_k D$. The conclusion is that the image of the subalgebra $\{a \in A \mid (\mathrm{id} \otimes \mu)(\Delta a) = a \otimes 1\}$ under $\pi$ is exactly $\{b \in B \mid (\mathrm{id} \otimes \rho)(\Delta b) = b \otimes 1\}$.
--
--   In the language of finite commutative group schemes over $k$, with $G = \operatorname{Spec} A$, $H = \operatorname{Spec} B$, $N = \operatorname{Spec} C$ and $H \cap N = \operatorname{Spec} D$, this is the second isomorphism theorem: the induced map $\mathcal{O}(G/N) \to \mathcal{O}(H/(H \cap N))$ is surjective, so that $H/(H \cap N) \to G/N$ is a closed immersion. It is used in the analysis of towers of finite flat group schemes, in particular for the comparison of Hopf kernels along the transition maps of such a tower and in the surjectivity statements for the associated Witt-coordinate maps.
-- source:
